From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ahmedawachi <121810925+ahmedawachi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 10:08:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] networking: split large plain replies over nodes of at
 most 256 KB

What: _addReplyPayloadToList() can split a plain (copied) payload over
several reply list nodes. Each new node is still at least
PROTO_REPLY_CHUNK_BYTES (16 KB) and at most reply-node-max-bytes of
buffer. With the Cygwin/MSYS2 default of 256 KB - 64, a 1.87 MB hash
field becomes eight nodes of about 256 KB instead of one 1.87 MB
allocation. Copy-avoidance nodes (BULK_STR_REF) are unchanged: their
payload is a small reference.

reply-node-max-bytes is a hidden config: 0 (no split) or at least
16 KB; values in between are refused. It defaults to 256 KB - 64 on
Cygwin/MSYS2 builds and to 0 everywhere else, so Linux and macOS builds
keep upstream's allocation pattern. Fake clients (Lua,
modules, AOF loading) are never split: their reply is concatenated
afterwards anyway.

Behaviour is otherwise unchanged:
- Accounting: every node still adds its size to reply_bytes, and
  closeClientOnOutputBufferLimitReached() still runs once, after the
  whole payload has been queued, so client-output-buffer-limit sees the
  same total as before.
- Writing: the nodes after the first carry a new buf_continued flag
  (it fits in the padding of clientReplyBlock, whose size stays 24
  bytes on LP64). _writevToClient() adds such continuation nodes to the
  same writev() even past NET_MAX_WRITES_PER_EVENT, so a split reply is
  offered to the socket exactly as the single large node was. Without
  this, each event would write only the first 256 KB: a 1.9 MB HGET
  would take 8 writes and 8 event-loop cycles instead of 1, and on
  Cygwin every extra cycle is a select() round trip.
- The bytes on the wire are identical.

Why: a production crash of the MSYS2 build was exactly this allocation:
zmalloc_usable(1868918 + 24) for an HMGET of a 1.87 MB hash field, which
Windows refused to commit while the dataset was only 64 MB. Hash values
do not use the zero-copy reply path, so the whole value was copied into
one contiguous block. The 256 KB bound keeps each node below the 256 KB
mmap threshold of the Cygwin/MSYS2 malloc (dlmalloc 2.8.6), including
the 24-byte node header, the 8-byte zmalloc prefix and dlmalloc's chunk
overhead, so reply nodes come from the heap and reuse freed chunks
instead of asking Windows for a fresh commit each time.

This does not remove every large allocation. The stored value and the
query buffer of each big HSET still need about 1.9 MB contiguous (the
next patch makes the query buffer failure non-fatal).

Tests: tests/unit/win-soft-oom.tcl. The default is on only for
Cygwin/MSYS2; values below 16 KB are refused; with the cap off a 2 MB
reply stays in one node; with it on, 2 MB HMGET, HGET and GETRANGE
replies (and GET, which uses copy avoidance and is never split) arrive
byte for byte, CLIENT INFO
inside MULTI shows oll >= 8 and omem within oll * 256 KB, pipelined
large replies keep their order, and a 1 MB client-output-buffer-limit
still disconnects the client.

Risk: low. More list nodes for replies above 256 KB on Cygwin/MSYS2
builds only.
